    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Compares adaptive optimizers on held-out translation and ImageNet metrics, using a different optimizer and task setup from the versions' cited studies.",
    "gate_note": "MNIST and CIFAR-10 training loss/error results do not measure a train–test gap or held-out performance, so they are not rows. The paper's Reddi Problem results concern synthetic optimization convergence, not the claim's natural-label generalization. Cited results from other work are not rows.",
    "test_reason": "The study compares adaptive optimizers with one another, without an SGD baseline. Its held-out results therefore do not test V4's adaptive-versus-SGD claim or its tuning, weight-decay, or switching qualifications. None of its other experiments tests V1–V3 or V5–V8.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is substantially accurate. The authors conclude that Expectigrad combines convergence guarantees with competitive empirical performance across architectures. They describe Adam's translation failure as divergence in the conclusion, but Section 6 explicitly treats its proposed connection to rare gradients and the Reddi Problem as speculation requiring further evidence.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Tests Expectigrad against other adaptive methods on translation and ImageNet held-out metrics, rather than the SGD comparisons and tuning conditions in V4; no direct train–test gap analysis.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Task and optimizer; reported test outcomes differ across LSTM and Transformer translation tasks and optimizer choice.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "With fixed shared translation hyperparameters and no search, Adam produced near-zero test BLEU on both architectures, whereas the other adaptive methods achieved substantially higher scores.",
        "proposed_claim": "On these IWSLT’15 translation recipes, optimizer choice among adaptive methods strongly affects held-out BLEU; the results do not distinguish optimization failure from a generalization disadvantage.",
